[Music]  [Music]  the triforce has become one of the most  famous video game symbols of all time  imagine that putting three triangles  together and pretty much everyone out  there knows exactly where it comes from  the british third army division founded  in 1809 by arthur wellesley first duke  of wellington  ok yeah nintendo definitely wasn't the  first to come up with the design but in  the world of gaming it has become  synonymous with the zelda series but  where did the inspiration for the  triforce come from i have a particular  gripe with this subject and it's not  because it's still a bit of a mystery to  this day but in fact that people pretend  that it isn't i've been wanting to do a  video about the origins of the triforce  for a while now and essentially canceled  the idea because well the mystery was  already solved or so i was led to  believe but after a bit of digging you  quickly realize that nintendo has never  outright confirmed anything when it  comes to where they got the idea from  the conclusion presented is simply a  best guess and not a solid fact at all a  very good best guess mind you but i've  always felt there was something missing  so before we get into what the main  consensus is let's do a little  backtracking first  1986 nintendo releases the first game in  the zelda franchise simply titled the  legend of zelda the game itself barely  showcased any story at all except for a  very brief introduction text which can  only be seen if you leave the title  screen on idle for about 10 seconds the  only other piece of story exposition was  saved for the game's manual two whole  pages in fact by the way great choice of  text color and background guys i guess  this was before readability became a  thing anyway back then there were only  two triforce pieces power and wisdom  power being in the possession of ganon  and wisdom having been separated into  eight pieces and hidden across hyrule by  zelda nowhere does it ever state that a  third triforce piece exists it just says  the legend of the triforce golden  triangles possessing mystical powers and  on page 10 it reads link has destroyed  ganon and has managed to rescue princess  zelda now that he's got a hold of the  two triforces he once again goes on his  travels towards countries unknown notice  also how they use the word triforces  plural to describe the two triangles  nowadays they usually don't do that  anymore and all three pieces combined  are referred to as the triforce whereas  individually they are either called a  triforce piece or simply by their virtue  like triforce of power or triforce of  wisdom oh and just in case you're  wondering the japanese version is almost  identical the biggest difference being  that here it's not the legend of the  triforces that's being passed down but  the objects themselves what followed was  zelda 2 the adventure of link this game  shows the first depiction of the  triforce symbol as we know it today but  not in the actual game itself oh no it  barely misses the mark on that one  the only time we see it as one entity is  again in the game's manual and not even  the relic itself but in the form of a  mark on the back of link's hand this is  the first time that a third piece of the  triforce is mentioned and obtaining it  is the main goal of the story but i said  before the three pieces never combined  into a single entity in game and remain  disconnected from each other then came a  link to the past and the first time we  see the triforce in its full glory  suddenly the symbol as we know it today  was all over the place as soon as you  boot up the game the logo prologue on  shields statues in the ending cutscene  and of course in the manual it's also  here that we see the first iteration of  a backstory for the triforce in zelda 1  and 2 this was never touched upon it  just said it had mystical powers but  didn't explain where they came from or  what these powers are exactly a link to  the past essentially cemented to  triforce as the three-part wish-granting  all-powerful relic created by the gods  we are all familiar with and from here  on out this narrative would largely  remain the same with some extra lore  being added here and there so with that  said what is the main consensus when it  comes to the inspiration behind the  triforce well in a nutshell its origin  can be traced back to the family crest  or mon from the hojo clan it's a family  that played a crucial role in medieval  japan most famous for their successful  opposition to the mongol invasions of  japan and fostering zen buddhism across  the country the crest itself originates  from a legend where the head of the  family tokimasa hojo went to visit a  dragon deity on the island of enoshima  to pray for the prosperity of his  descendants the dragon answered and left  behind three scales which together  formed the mitsuroko which translates to  three scales this legend may have also  been the inspiration for the three  dragons in breath of the wild as they  too leave behind the scale but i digress  up until the zelda series brought the  triforce into our pop culture the symbol  of the mitsuroko was relatively obscure  in the west but in japan it has  persisted for centuries many families  adapted a similar symbol as their crest  and it can still be found in japanese  graveyards today including the grave of  gunpei yokoi inventor of the original  gameboy who tragically died in a car  accident in 1997. he's also described as  having been a mentor for suguru miyamoto  the visionary for super mario and the  zelda series this has caused some to  believe that the inclusion of the  triforce symbol was an homage to his  mentor who would have still been alive  at that point as he died only a mere  month before the release of ocarina of  time there's also an important deity  named ben zaitan who serves as both a  buddhist goddess as well as a shinto  kami there are multiple shrines devoted  to her including in and around the city  of kyoto some of which depict a  triforce-like symbol as well and  miyamoto has expressed that he likes to  visit these kinds of shrines aside from  that the mitsuroko is also found in the  corporate world like the japanese energy  company mitsuroko so yeah miyamoto would  have no doubt been well aware of the  existence of this icon as it's part of  japanese culture and history but as far  as i'm aware he's never publicly stated  whether he took the inspiration straight  from the history of the hojo family or  if it was an homage to yokoi's family  crest or if he just simply looked at his  energy bill one day and was like yes  either way it doesn't really matter as  ultimately it all links back to the hojo  legend some claim he did make a  statement about it and if that's the  case please provide me with a source i  have looked everywhere and i cannot find  it so given all i've just outlined i  think it's safe to say that yes the  mitsuroko was most likely the  inspiration for the triforce as we know  it today the case closed right well  here's the thing though while it does  explain why the triforce looks the way  it does now is this really the true  origin of the relic because remember the  triforce didn't show up until zelda 2's  manual and didn't make a full-on  appearance in-game until a link to the  past if the idea for the triforce was  always there and based on the mitsuroko  symbol why was it completely absent from  the first zelda and not just from the  game itself but promotional art  commercials and anything else related to  the game well i believe that initially  there may have been a different  inspiration for the triforce one that  would later be changed as the series  continued let me demonstrate what i mean  although the zelda series was created in  japan and by a japanese company it's  become obvious that the series was  initially inspired by a lot of western  ideas and catered towards a western  audience we can see this fact pop up  again and again in the game itself  artwork interviews with the series  creators and even the music the first  and obvious one that most people know by  now is that the name of the princess as  well as the series as a whole is named  after american writer and painter zelda  fitzgerald because miyamoto thought the  name sounded elegant but that's just the  tip of the iceberg in the nes original  the book of magic resembles a standard  depiction of the holy book of  christianity the bible with a cross on  the cover and in the japanese version of  the game the book of magic is literally  called bible furthermore we find many  cross symbolism on shields enemies and  graves all throughout the first two  games and zelda 2 the adventure of link  even sees the cross as a quest item it  allows link to see enemies which appear  as invisible without it and navigate the  valley of death another clear reference  to the christian faith this also applies  to ganon himself nowadays he's mostly  described as the demon king or king of  evil or something along those lines but  back then he was referred to as the  prince of darkness which is one of many  titles commonly used to address the  devil what's weird is that they didn't  censor this in the opening crawl but did  remove it in some parts of the manual  for some reason even hyrule itself its  culture and architecture is confirmed to  be based on medieval europe as stated by  hidemaru fujibayashi the opening title  music of the first game was originally  supposed to be an 8-bit rendition of a  classical song called bolero by french  composer maurice revell however right  before the release of zelda 1 it turned  out that the song was still copyrighted  and thus the series composer koji kondo  pulled an all-nighter to compose an  original song which came to be the  iconic zelda theme we all know and love  during an interview to celebrate the  release of the nes classic miyamoto even  made the remark that at the time the  song reminded him of spaghetti western  movies speaking of miyamoto in the same  interview he was also asked why he  decided to make the legend of zelda  series at the time and his answer was  simply the indiana jones movies were out  at the time and continues on to say that  he wanted to bring that sense of  adventure to a video game if you know  anything about the indiana jones movies  you know that it features a lot of  western mythology particularly  christianity like the ark of the  covenant the cross of coronado the holy  grail and so on basically what i'm  saying is that miyamoto has since the  beginning of his career been inspired by  these kinds of themes and the same goes  for takashi tezuka he is i guess one of  the lesser known figures from the zelda  development history but equally as  important as he was the director and is  considered to be the father of the zelda  storyline in the early 2d era prior to  zelda he and miyamoto made a game called  devil world which was never released in  the u.s for  obvious reasons it too features holy  crosses and well  the devil safe to say that the two were  definitely no stranger to taking  inspiration from this kind of subject  matter it's actually a bit ironic if you  think about it nintendo tried to appeal  to a western audience by putting in all  sorts of references to their most  prominent religion but it sort of had  the opposite effect and a lot of it  ended up getting censored to avoid  controversy even to this day nintendo of  america still removes or changes certain  words or phrases especially things  regarding demons or the demon tribe  check out this video by quest with aaron  if you want to see just how much the  english translation differs in some  cases nowadays a lot of people refer to  shintoism as one of the more prominent  influences for the series lore but that  didn't really start becoming a  noticeable thing until later which is  why i find it a bit difficult to believe  that the triforce was always meant to be  based on japanese mythology even though  almost everything else was clearly based  on christianity to some degree there  aren't a lot of quotes from the game's  developers about the triforce in the nes  era though miyamoto has revealed one  interesting thing about it since namely  that the original zelda was supposed to  feature elements of science fiction the  pieces of the triforce link collects  were meant to be microchips and link  would travel between the past and the  future and not like ocarina of time and  its seven year time gap but like the  matrix type of future and he closes by  saying but in the end link never went to  the future and it remained a heroic  fantasy game we can even say that there  was absolutely nothing futuristic  another example of the uncertainty that  existed back in the late 80s regarding  what exactly the triforce was supposed  to be is found in the legend of zelda  cartoon from 1998. of course this isn't  canon to the series lore but it is  obvious that the creators of the show  try to interpret and adapt as much of  the original source material as they  possibly could from link's abilities to  the sound effects the enemies ganon etc  and when it comes to the triforce you  can see that there wasn't really a good  understanding yet regarding its origin  abilities and overall lore here too only  two parts of the triforce exist one in  zelda's possession and one in the hands  of ganon similar to the first game and  the entire plot revolves around ganon  trying to get his hands on both and  conquer hyrule they don't resemble  anything close to the modern day  triforce at all and are instead upside  down pyramids and possess the power of  speech it's also worth mentioning that  although the series was made and  published by a third party it was  overseen by nintendo of america so let's  say the first iteration of the triforce  wasn't based on the hojo legend and the  mitsuroko symbolism then what's the  alternative given the track record of  influences from christianity in the  early games i'd say this might apply to  the triforce as well obviously i cannot  prove anything since there are simply no  sources to fall back on but my best  guess is that it may have been based on  the equilateral triangle a symbol  associated with the holy trinity of the  father the son and the holy spirit i do  have to mention that this depiction  isn't universal throughout all of  christianity and not all depictions of  this are still accepted today it kind of  depends on who you ask and which branch  of christianity you would use as a  reference but in some cases imagery of  one or more golden triangles are used to  represent the trinity so i don't think  it's out of the realm of possibility  that someone from the original zelda  team whether it was miyamoto or tezuka  took inspiration from these depictions  it would fall in line with all the other  christian references and the triforce  was sort of a god-like relic with  mystical powers a more boring  alternative would be that the triforce  took on the shape of a yellow triangle  because it was simply an easy shape to  use given the graphical limitations at  the time i mean there's only so many  primitives you can choose from so maybe  it was just a random choice which  coincidentally fit well with the later  ideas behind the triforce one could of  course make the case that nintendo chose  a triangle because they were always  planning to someday merge three of them  together to form the mitsuroku symbol  which is possible the original creators  were hoping that they could continue the  series after the first game but at the  same time i just don't see nintendo  planning a road map that far ahead i  mean the zelda timeline is kind of a  testament to that especially considering  they couldn't have known how big and  successful the zelda series would become  in the future the original lore was  vague and lacked a lot of exposition and  backstory if there was an elaborate plan  for the series laura at this point i  would honestly be very impressed as the  series progressed nintendo slowly  started doing away with a lot of  real-life religious references in favor  of their own original lore as well as  elements from japanese mythology this  became especially noticeable with  skyward sword and recently breath of the  wild and it's definitely not an uncommon  thing to do as an example miyazaki also  started his career being heavily  inspired by european architecture and  settings only to bring in more and more  japanese influences later on zelda was  intended to be a classic medieval  adventure story with a lot of  inspiration from western mythology and  religion but slowly veered off into its  own direction as it became increasingly  successful of course everything we've  discussed here today is in regards to  the aesthetics of the triforce when it  comes to its actual inner workings and  mythology well  that is a whole different story  altogether there's definitely a lot to  explore here as well but that's too big  for one video and perhaps i will dig  into this as well someday as always i  highly encourage a good and healthy  discussion in the comments below if you  have your own ideas or found interesting  details about the subject don't shy away  from sharing them with me and others or  if you're just here to chime in about  anything else zelda related or just here  to say hi that is of course more than  welcome too a big thanks to you for  watching to my mods both on discord and  my streams and of course to my patreons  and channel members who remain ever so  generous oh and apparently my channel  has been blessed with a new beta  functionality which allows you to make a  one-time donation a bit like a super  chat but for an individual video so if  you want to support the channel  financially but you don't want to commit  to a membership this is a nice  alternative though never feel obligated  to do so your support as a viewer is  already greatly appreciated and that is  all for now this is dawn signing off and  have a good one  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you

